{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "query": "I'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.", "system": "You are the blind evaluator-expectation scout for a research system. You receive\nthe original request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output.\nPredict the small set of atomic, binary checks that a demanding knowledgeable\nreader would use to distinguish a complete answer from a merely plausible one.\nDo not answer the request, state facts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. Do not write broad wishlist items: each\naddition must express one independently checkable obligation. Check for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one; when the request joins\n  two central variables or populations, require at least one prior measurement\n  that studies that combination, not two unrelated adjacent studies;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- the exact proper name, acronym, governing law or standard, canonical method,\n  dataset, comparison baseline, or metric that generic prose would otherwise\n  paraphrase away; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: rank atomic checks by expected\n  evaluation value per answer word and prefer coverage over optional depth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument. For a recursively self-improving system, explicitly recall\n  escalation or intelligence-feedback dynamics, concealed-cooperation or\n  deceptive-turn scenarios, and instrumental incentives alongside formal\n  self-verification limits.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request. For each selected check, put any term that must be\nstated literally in `must_mention`; use an empty list when no exact term is\nneeded. A search phrase is not an obligation: if HIPAA, a named theorem, a\nregulator, or another standard matters, name it in both the requirement and\n`must_mention`. When sensitive records, protected attributes, health, law, or\nanother regulated domain is present, one `safety` obligation naming the\napplicable law or standard is mandatory and displaces the lowest-ranked format\nitem. For a society-wide, medical, or causal judgment, include one `penalty`\nobligation naming the specific unsupported generalization to avoid. Include at\nmost two penalty-avoidance checks total.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"term|evidence|mechanism|comparison|scope|safety|format|penalty\",\"requirement\":\"one atomic operational requirement\",\"must_mention\":[\"exact term\"],\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"one compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most eight additions, ranked by expected evaluation weight per answer\nword. Keep each requirement under 30 words, each reason under 35 words, at most\nthree exact terms, and at most one search lead. Do not combine distinct checks\nwith a list joined by \"and\". Use verdict \"pass\" only when no material implicit\ncheck exists. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"scope\",\"requirement\":\"Identify the exact CDC WONDER database, years, race coding scheme, subgroup labels, suppressed cells, and whether it supplies outcomes or denominator data.\",\"must_mention\":[\"CDC WONDER\"],\"reason\":\"WONDER tables differ in racial granularity and purpose; an unspecified table cannot support a defensible subgroup crosswalk or prevalence estimate.\",\"search_leads\":[\"site:wonder.cdc.gov Asian subgroup race categories data documentation\"]},{\"kind\":\"evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Cite at least one prior study measuring autoimmune disease prevalence within disaggregated Asian populations, rather than separate studies of race extraction and autoimmune disease.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"A directly relevant precedent grounds subgroup selection, disease definitions, expected sample sizes, and interpretation of heterogeneity.\",\"search_leads\":[\"disaggregated Asian subgroup autoimmune disease prevalence study\"]},{\"kind\":\"mechanism\",\"requirement\":\"Benchmark rule-based extraction, named-entity recognition, and supervised classification against manually reviewed records using subgroup-specific precision, recall, and F1.\",\"must_mention\":[\"precision\",\"recall\",\"F1\"],\"reason\":\"The project needs concrete NLP alternatives plus a gold-standard validation strategy, especially because performance may differ across rare subgroups.\",\"search_leads\":[\"EHR race ethnicity NLP chart review validation\"]},{\"kind\":\"mechanism\",\"requirement\":\"Define the prevalence estimand using a validated disease phenotype, eligible-patient denominator, observation window, and handling of repeated encounters.\",\"must_mention\":[\"ICD-10-CM\",\"SNOMED CT\",\"point prevalence\"],\"reason\":\"Counting diagnoses or mentions without a denominator and time definition produces encounter frequency, not interpretable prevalence.\",\"search_leads\":[\"EHR computable phenotype autoimmune disease prevalence denominator\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"Show one synthetic EHR example containing structured demographics and a de-identified clinical note, then demonstrate the intended subgroup extraction output.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"The requester explicitly lacks familiarity with EHRs; a worked example makes the data sources, NLP input, and abstraction target concrete.\",\"search_leads\":[\"synthetic EHR structured fields clinical note example\"]},{\"kind\":\"safety\",\"requirement\":\"Specify approval and data-handling checks under the HIPAA Privacy Rule, including institutional review and restrictions on re-identification or small-cell disclosure.\",\"must_mention\":[\"HIPAA Privacy Rule\",\"IRB\"],\"reason\":\"De-identified health records remain institutionally governed, and rare ethnicity-by-disease cells can create privacy risks.\",\"search_leads\":[\"HIPAA de-identified EHR research IRB small cell disclosure\"]},{\"kind\":\"penalty\",\"requirement\":\"Do not equate surname, language, birthplace, or clinician perception with self-identified race/ethnicity without validation and an explicit bias analysis.\",\"must_mention\":[\"self-identified race/ethnicity\"],\"reason\":\"Proxy-based assignment can misclassify multiracial, adopted, immigrant, and linguistically diverse patients, creating unsupported subgroup disparities.\",\"search_leads\":[\"surname ethnicity inference EHR bias validation\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"Provide a staged research plan mapping prerequisite skills, data access, annotation, modeling, validation, prevalence analysis, and professor-confirmation checkpoints to concrete deliverables.\",\"must_mention\":[\"Python\",\"SQL\",\"Git\"],\"reason\":\"The user requested both a skills roadmap and an actionable plan; milestones prevent premature modeling before taxonomy, access, and labeling decisions are settled.\",\"search_leads\":[\"clinical NLP EHR research workflow skills annotation validation\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"kind": "scope", "requirement": "Identify the exact CDC WONDER database, years, race coding scheme, subgroup labels, suppressed cells, and whether it supplies outcomes or denominator data.", "must_mention": ["CDC WONDER"], "reason": "WONDER tables differ in racial granularity and purpose; an unspecified table cannot support a defensible subgroup crosswalk or prevalence estimate.", "search_leads": ["site:wonder.cdc.gov Asian subgroup race categories data documentation"]}, {"kind": "evidence", "requirement": "Cite at least one prior study measuring autoimmune disease prevalence within disaggregated Asian populations, rather than separate studies of race extraction and autoimmune disease.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "A directly relevant precedent grounds subgroup selection, disease definitions, expected sample sizes, and interpretation of heterogeneity.", "search_leads": ["disaggregated Asian subgroup autoimmune disease prevalence study"]}, {"kind": "mechanism", "requirement": "Benchmark rule-based extraction, named-entity recognition, and supervised classification against manually reviewed records using subgroup-specific precision, recall, and F1.", "must_mention": ["precision", "recall", "F1"], "reason": "The project needs concrete NLP alternatives plus a gold-standard validation strategy, especially because performance may differ across rare subgroups.", "search_leads": ["EHR race ethnicity NLP chart review validation"]}, {"kind": "mechanism", "requirement": "Define the prevalence estimand using a validated disease phenotype, eligible-patient denominator, observation window, and handling of repeated encounters.", "must_mention": ["ICD-10-CM", "SNOMED CT", "point prevalence"], "reason": "Counting diagnoses or mentions without a denominator and time definition produces encounter frequency, not interpretable prevalence.", "search_leads": ["EHR computable phenotype autoimmune disease prevalence denominator"]}, {"kind": "format", "requirement": "Show one synthetic EHR example containing structured demographics and a de-identified clinical note, then demonstrate the intended subgroup extraction output.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "The requester explicitly lacks familiarity with EHRs; a worked example makes the data sources, NLP input, and abstraction target concrete.", "search_leads": ["synthetic EHR structured fields clinical note example"]}, {"kind": "safety", "requirement": "Specify approval and data-handling checks under the HIPAA Privacy Rule, including institutional review and restrictions on re-identification or small-cell disclosure.", "must_mention": ["HIPAA Privacy Rule", "IRB"], "reason": "De-identified health records remain institutionally governed, and rare ethnicity-by-disease cells can create privacy risks.", "search_leads": ["HIPAA de-identified EHR research IRB small cell disclosure"]}, {"kind": "penalty", "requirement": "Do not equate surname, language, birthplace, or clinician perception with self-identified race/ethnicity without validation and an explicit bias analysis.", "must_mention": ["self-identified race/ethnicity"], "reason": "Proxy-based assignment can misclassify multiracial, adopted, immigrant, and linguistically diverse patients, creating unsupported subgroup disparities.", "search_leads": ["surname ethnicity inference EHR bias validation"]}, {"kind": "format", "requirement": "Provide a staged research plan mapping prerequisite skills, data access, annotation, modeling, validation, prevalence analysis, and professor-confirmation checkpoints to concrete deliverables.", "must_mention": ["Python", "SQL", "Git"], "reason": "The user requested both a skills roadmap and an actionable plan; milestones prevent premature modeling before taxonomy, access, and labeling decisions are settled.", "search_leads": ["clinical NLP EHR research workflow skills annotation validation"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1287, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 2247}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "query": "Conduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.", "system": "You are the blind evaluator-expectation scout for a research system. You receive\nthe original request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output.\nPredict the small set of atomic, binary checks that a demanding knowledgeable\nreader would use to distinguish a complete answer from a merely plausible one.\nDo not answer the request, state facts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. Do not write broad wishlist items: each\naddition must express one independently checkable obligation. Check for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one; when the request joins\n  two central variables or populations, require at least one prior measurement\n  that studies that combination, not two unrelated adjacent studies;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- the exact proper name, acronym, governing law or standard, canonical method,\n  dataset, comparison baseline, or metric that generic prose would otherwise\n  paraphrase away; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: rank atomic checks by expected\n  evaluation value per answer word and prefer coverage over optional depth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument. For a recursively self-improving system, explicitly recall\n  escalation or intelligence-feedback dynamics, concealed-cooperation or\n  deceptive-turn scenarios, and instrumental incentives alongside formal\n  self-verification limits.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request. For each selected check, put any term that must be\nstated literally in `must_mention`; use an empty list when no exact term is\nneeded. A search phrase is not an obligation: if HIPAA, a named theorem, a\nregulator, or another standard matters, name it in both the requirement and\n`must_mention`. When sensitive records, protected attributes, health, law, or\nanother regulated domain is present, one `safety` obligation naming the\napplicable law or standard is mandatory and displaces the lowest-ranked format\nitem. For a society-wide, medical, or causal judgment, include one `penalty`\nobligation naming the specific unsupported generalization to avoid. Include at\nmost two penalty-avoidance checks total.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"term|evidence|mechanism|comparison|scope|safety|format|penalty\",\"requirement\":\"one atomic operational requirement\",\"must_mention\":[\"exact term\"],\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"one compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most eight additions, ranked by expected evaluation weight per answer\nword. Keep each requirement under 30 words, each reason under 35 words, at most\nthree exact terms, and at most one search lead. Do not combine distinct checks\nwith a list joined by \"and\". Use verdict \"pass\" only when no material implicit\ncheck exists. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"scope\",\"requirement\":\"Use a multilevel framework covering individual well-being, social relationships, public institutions, education, public health, and economic life.\",\"must_mention\":[\"individual well-being\",\"public institutions\",\"economic life\"],\"reason\":\"A society-wide verdict is incomplete if it selects only conspicuous outcomes such as mental health or elections.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media societal impact domain framework review\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"For every domain, include at least one academic study, one journalistic report, and one concrete case study.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"The request repeats this evidence requirement per domain; satisfying it only across the essay would be noncompliant.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media impacts systematic review case studies journalism\"]},{\"kind\":\"scope\",\"requirement\":\"Define social media, the geographic and temporal boundaries, and the counterfactual baseline underlying the net-impact judgment.\",\"must_mention\":[\"counterfactual\"],\"reason\":\"An overall verdict changes depending on platform type, era, population, geography, and comparison with a world without social media.\",\"search_leads\":[\"defining social media platforms historical periods counterfactual impact\"]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"Apply one explicit weighting rubric using effect magnitude, population reach, causal confidence, distribution, and time horizon across domains.\",\"must_mention\":[\"causal confidence\"],\"reason\":\"A definitive judgment requires commensurable criteria rather than informally counting positive and negative examples.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social impact assessment weighting magnitude reach causality distribution\"]},{\"kind\":\"mechanism\",\"requirement\":\"Explain how platform design mediates outcomes through recommender systems, engagement optimization, and network effects.\",\"must_mention\":[\"recommender systems\",\"engagement optimization\",\"network effects\"],\"reason\":\"Treating social media as a uniform exposure obscures why benefits and harms vary across platforms and technological eras.\",\"search_leads\":[\"recommender systems engagement optimization network effects social outcomes\"]},{\"kind\":\"evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Include at least one non-Western case directly measuring social media alongside a specified societal outcome.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Evidence concentrated in Western populations cannot support an unqualified society-wide conclusion.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media societal outcomes Global South empirical study\"]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"Contrast evidence under the EU Digital Services Act with the U.S. Section 230 regime.\",\"must_mention\":[\"Digital Services Act\",\"Section 230\"],\"reason\":\"Regulatory context materially changes platform incentives, accountability, moderation, and the generalizability of observed effects.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Digital Services Act Section 230 platform governance comparison\"]},{\"kind\":\"penalty\",\"requirement\":\"Avoid claiming social media causes population-wide mental-health deterioration solely from cross-sectional correlations or self-reported screen time.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"This common generalization conflates association with causation and ignores substantial heterogeneity by age, vulnerability, use pattern, and platform.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media mental health causal evidence cross-sectional screen time critique\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"kind": "scope", "requirement": "Use a multilevel framework covering individual well-being, social relationships, public institutions, education, public health, and economic life.", "must_mention": ["individual well-being", "public institutions", "economic life"], "reason": "A society-wide verdict is incomplete if it selects only conspicuous outcomes such as mental health or elections.", "search_leads": ["social media societal impact domain framework review"]}, {"kind": "format", "requirement": "For every domain, include at least one academic study, one journalistic report, and one concrete case study.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "The request repeats this evidence requirement per domain; satisfying it only across the essay would be noncompliant.", "search_leads": ["social media impacts systematic review case studies journalism"]}, {"kind": "scope", "requirement": "Define social media, the geographic and temporal boundaries, and the counterfactual baseline underlying the net-impact judgment.", "must_mention": ["counterfactual"], "reason": "An overall verdict changes depending on platform type, era, population, geography, and comparison with a world without social media.", "search_leads": ["defining social media platforms historical periods counterfactual impact"]}, {"kind": "comparison", "requirement": "Apply one explicit weighting rubric using effect magnitude, population reach, causal confidence, distribution, and time horizon across domains.", "must_mention": ["causal confidence"], "reason": "A definitive judgment requires commensurable criteria rather than informally counting positive and negative examples.", "search_leads": ["social impact assessment weighting magnitude reach causality distribution"]}, {"kind": "mechanism", "requirement": "Explain how platform design mediates outcomes through recommender systems, engagement optimization, and network effects.", "must_mention": ["recommender systems", "engagement optimization", "network effects"], "reason": "Treating social media as a uniform exposure obscures why benefits and harms vary across platforms and technological eras.", "search_leads": ["recommender systems engagement optimization network effects social outcomes"]}, {"kind": "evidence", "requirement": "Include at least one non-Western case directly measuring social media alongside a specified societal outcome.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "Evidence concentrated in Western populations cannot support an unqualified society-wide conclusion.", "search_leads": ["social media societal outcomes Global South empirical study"]}, {"kind": "comparison", "requirement": "Contrast evidence under the EU Digital Services Act with the U.S. Section 230 regime.", "must_mention": ["Digital Services Act", "Section 230"], "reason": "Regulatory context materially changes platform incentives, accountability, moderation, and the generalizability of observed effects.", "search_leads": ["Digital Services Act Section 230 platform governance comparison"]}, {"kind": "penalty", "requirement": "Avoid claiming social media causes population-wide mental-health deterioration solely from cross-sectional correlations or self-reported screen time.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "This common generalization conflates association with causation and ignores substantial heterogeneity by age, vulnerability, use pattern, and platform.", "search_leads": ["social media mental health causal evidence cross-sectional screen time critique"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1199, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 2871}}
